  "question": {
    "text": "Which transition in the hydrogen spectrum would have the same wavelength as the Balmer type transition from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>He</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>spectrum"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>For&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>He</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;ion, the wave number associated with the Balmer transition,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;is given by:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>RZ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>-</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup></mstyle></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>4</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></math>&nbsp;= atomic number of helium</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#955;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>According to the question, the desired transition for hydrogen&nbsp;will have the same wavelength as that of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>He</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mfenced><msup><mi>He</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo>)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>By hit and trial method,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math></p><p>On comparing&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>So, the correct answer is n = 2 to n = 1.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
  }
}